== the code-signing block as it now reads ==
$ sed -n '64,101p' .gitignore

# Code signing material - never commit any of it.
# android/.gitignore already covers key.properties and Android keystores; these
# patterns cover the Apple side and re-cover keystores from the repo root,
# because .github/workflows/deploy-ios.yml's (commented) signing step writes
# certificate.p12 into the working directory, which is the repo root.
*.p12
*.p8
*.mobileprovision
*.provisionprofile
*.cer
*.certSigningRequest
*.keystore
*.jks
# Private key material in its commonest raw forms (koniz-dev/flutter-starter#164).
# An exported signing key, a service-account key, a TLS key and an SSH key all
# land as a .pem or a .key; .pfx is the same PKCS#12 container as the .p12
# above, under its Windows name. tool/check_env_assets.dart already refuses to
# let any of them be declared as a Flutter asset
# (koniz-dev/flutter-starter#138), so git should not track one either. That
# guard's `secretAssetExtensions` set and this block are now the same list of
# private formats, which is the point: one policy, two enforcement points.
*.pem
*.key
*.pfx

# NOT ignored, on purpose: *.crt and *.der.
# A certificate is the public half of a key pair. Certificate pinning
# legitimately ships one inside the app, so ignoring them would break a
# supported setup for no gain. koniz-dev/flutter-starter#138 decided this - see
# the "Public certificates" note at the top of tool/check_env_assets.dart,
# which leaves .cer, .crt and .der out of its secret set for the same reason -
# and koniz-dev/flutter-starter#164 kept it. If you are here to "fix the
# inconsistency", this is the reason not to.
#
# `*.cer` is the one asymmetry, and it is deliberate too: it predates both
# issues, arriving in dbee1c6 with the Apple signing block above, where a .cer
# turns up as one step of a private-key workflow rather than as a pinning
